---
import FAQSection from "@components/article/FAQSection.astro";
import RelatedArticles from "@components/article/RelatedArticles.astro";
import ArticleLayout from "../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";

// Article metadata
const title = "Essential Travel Tech for Digital Nomads (2025)";
const description =
  "Worked from forty countries. Here's the compact tech that earns its weight in my backpack, what flopped after two months, and what I never travel without.";
const authorSlug = "daniel-brooks";
const pubDate = new Date("2025-02-14");
const readingTime = 10;
const image = "/images/unsplash/photo-1519389950473-47ba0277781c-1200w.webp";
const imageAlt = "Digital nomad travel tech setup with laptop and accessories";

// Table of contents
const headings = [
  { text: "My Bag Weighs 23 Pounds", slug: "introduction", depth: 2 },
  { text: "The Non-Negotiables", slug: "essentials", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Connectivity", slug: "connectivity", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Productivity Stuff", slug: "productivity", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Power", slug: "power", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Organization", slug: "comfort", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Quick Answers", slug: "faq", depth: 2 },
  { text: "What I've Learned", slug: "conclusion", depth: 2 },
];

// FAQ data
const faqs = [
  {
    question: "Phone hotspot or dedicated device?",
    answer:
      "<p>Phone works. Kills your battery though. I just keep my phone plugged in when I'm using it as a hotspot. Good enough.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Laptop specs?",
    answer:
      "<p>16GB RAM if you can. 8GB minimum. SSD required. Not negotiable. Battery life matters more than you think. Everything else is preference.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Portable monitor?",
    answer:
      "<p>Staying put for months? Maybe worth it. Actually traveling? Probably not. I tried. Didn't work for me.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Keeping gear safe?",
    answer:
      "<p>Never check electronics. Never leave stuff unattended. Get insurance that actually covers tech. Back everything up. Common sense mostly.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Adapters?",
    answer: "<p>One universal adapter with USB ports. Don't overthink it.</p>",
  },
];

// Related articles
const relatedArticles = [
  {
    title: "Best Laptops for Remote Work",
    excerpt: "Find the perfect laptop for productivity on the go.",
    href: "/gear/best-laptops-remote-work",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1496181133206-80ce9b88a853-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Laptop on desk",
  },
  {
    title: "Top Destinations for Digital Nomads",
    excerpt: "Cities with great WiFi, affordable living, and thriving communities.",
    href: "/blog/top-destinations-digital-nomads",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1528181304800-259b08848526-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Digital nomad destination",
  },
  {
    title: "How to Find Reliable WiFi Anywhere",
    excerpt: "Tips and tools for staying connected no matter where you travel.",
    href: "/blog/find-reliable-wifi-anywhere",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1521791136064-7986c2920216-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "WiFi signal concept",
  },
];
---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    authorSlug={authorSlug}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="Travel"
    categorySlug="guides/travel"
    headings={headings}
>
  <!-- Introduction -->
  <h2 id="introduction">My Bag Weighs 23 Pounds. Everything I Need Is In It.</h2>
  <p>
    Three years. Thirty-seven countries. And my packing list? Finally edited down to what I actually use.
  </p>
  <p>
    So here's a confession. I've bought so much garbage over the years. Stuff I was convinced I needed. The portable
    monitor that made total sense until I lugged it through two airports and thought "why am I doing this to myself."
    The mechanical keyboard that stayed in its case for four months straight. A backup charger I never once touched.
  </p>
  <p>
    All shipped home. Or thrown away. Or sold to some other nomad who probably did the same thing.
  </p>
  <p>
    Here's what actually survived.
  </p>

  <!-- The Essentials -->
  <h2 id="essentials">The Non-Negotiables</h2>

  <h3>Laptop: MacBook Air M3</h3>
  <p>
    Battery lasts all day. Not "most of the day" or "if you dim the screen". Actually all day. Silent because no fan.
    Light enough I forget it's there.
  </p>
  <p>
    Could I save money with Windows? Yeah probably. But I've been burned before. That ThinkPad I had in 2021 that would
    randomly freeze during <a href="/tools/best-video-conferencing-tools">client calls</a>? Never again. This thing just
    works. Three years, zero issues, zero thoughts
    about it. Worth every penny.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you want options though, we've got a <a href="/gear/best-laptops-remote-work">full laptop guide</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>Headphones: Sony WH-1000XM5</h3>
  <p>
    Cafes are loud. Airports are worse. And that hostel common room at 2am when some guy decides it's acoustic guitar
    time? Unbearable.
  </p>
  <p>
    These fix all of that. Instant quiet workspace anywhere.
  </p>
  <p>
    I've also got AirPods Pro for lighter days. Most nomads end up with both. You fight it for a while, then you just
    give in and buy both.
  </p>

  <h3>Mouse: Logitech MX Anywhere 3</h3>
  <p>
    Could just use the trackpad. I know. I choose not to.
  </p>
  <p>
    This works on any surface. Tested it on my jeans once out of desperation on a bus in Morocco. Worked fine. The
    trackpad would've been a nightmare.
  </p>

  <!-- Connectivity -->
  <h2 id="connectivity">Connectivity (The Stuff That Actually Matters)</h2>

  <h3>Airalo eSIM</h3>
  <p>
    Remember hunting for SIM card shops? Landing exhausted, dragging your bags around the airport, trying to mime "data
    plan" to someone who doesn't speak English?
  </p>
  <p>
    Yeah. Don't miss that.
  </p>
  <p>
    Now I buy data before I land. Two minutes. Done. Working internet the second I step off the plane. Should've been
    doing this years ago.
  </p>

  <h3>NordVPN</h3>
  <p>
    Cafe WiFi is basically a public bulletin board. Anyone with the right tools. Freely available online by the way. Can
    see what you're doing. Your bank login. Client emails. Everything.
  </p>
  <p>
    VPN scrambles all of it. Also lets me watch my Netflix from home when I'm homesick, which sounds dumb but matters at
    11pm in some random city when you just want something familiar.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="/tools/best-vpn-digital-nomads">Full VPN comparison here</a> if you want to compare options.
  </p>

  <h3>Anker USB-C Hub</h3>
  <p>
    My laptop has two USB-C ports. Two. Thanks Apple.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the hub gives me USB-A, HDMI, SD card when I need them. Use it maybe twice a week? Sometimes less. But when you
    need to plug into a projector for a presentation and realize you have no ports... yeah. Carry the hub.
  </p>

  <!-- Productivity Boosters -->
  <h2 id="productivity">Productivity Stuff</h2>

  <h3>Roost Laptop Stand</h3>
  <p>
    Folds flat. Weighs nothing. Puts my screen at eye level so my neck doesn't hate me.
  </p>
  <p>
    I resisted buying one forever. "I'm young, my posture is fine, I don't need that." Spoiler: my posture was not fine.
    My neck hurt constantly. Should've bought this day one.
  </p>

  <h3>Small Ring Light</h3>
  <p>
    Hotel room lighting is always terrible. Yellow and weird and makes you look exhausted even when you're not. Clients
    on video calls don't need to see that.
  </p>
  <p>
    Twenty dollar clip-on light. Fixed. They think I'm in some proper office setup. I'm sitting on a bed in Thailand.
  </p>

  <h3>No External Keyboard</h3>
  <p>
    Owned one. Carried it for months. Used it maybe five times? Not worth the weight. The MacBook keyboard is fine. Some
    people disagree. That's okay.
  </p>

  <h3>No Portable Monitor</h3>
  <p>
    Okay this one might be controversial.
  </p>
  <p>
    I bought one. Carried it around for three months being responsible. Used it four times. Sold it to another nomad in
    Lisbon.
  </p>
  <p>
    Here's the thing. If you're staying somewhere for months, different story. But if you're actually traveling, moving
    every few weeks? It's just dead weight. I thought I'd miss it. I don't.
  </p>

  <!-- Power Solutions -->
  <h2 id="power">Power</h2>

  <h3>Anker 735 Charger (65W)</h3>
  <p>
    One brick charges everything. Laptop. Phone. Headphones. Replaced three separate chargers I used to carry around
    like an idiot.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why did I wait so long for this? No idea.
  </p>

  <h3>Zendure Universal Adapter</h3>
  <p>
    Works everywhere. Every country I've tried. Has USB ports built in so you're not stacking adapter on adapter on
    adapter.
  </p>
  <p>
    Lost mine in Vietnam. Immediately ordered the exact same one again. That's the whole review.
  </p>

  <h3>Anker Power Bank (24,000mAh)</h3>
  <p>
    Long flights. Buses through the middle of nowhere. Cafes where every single outlet is already claimed.
  </p>
  <p>
    This thing has saved me more times than I can count. And it's juuust under the airline limit, which matters when
    you're on budget carriers that actually enforce the rules.
  </p>

  <!-- Travel Comfort -->
  <h2 id="comfort">Organization</h2>

  <h3>Peak Design Tech Pouch</h3>
  <p>
    Everything has a place. Cables in one spot. Adapters in another. Random small stuff. Pens, earbuds, that tiny
    screwdriver for my glasses. All organized.
  </p>
  <p>
    Can find anything instantly. Even half asleep at 5am in an airport. Yes it's expensive. Yes it's worth it. The cheap
    alternatives turn into tangled chaos within a week.
  </p>

  <h3>Cables I Actually Carry</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Two USB-C. One long for cafes where the outlet is weirdly far. One short for normal use.</li>
    <li>One Lightning for the AirPods</li>
    <li>One USB-A to USB-C for old ports</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    That's it. Four cables total. If I need something else I'll buy it locally. Haven't needed to yet.
  </p>

  <h3>Samsung T7 SSD</h3>
  <p>
    <a href="/tools/best-cloud-storage-remote-work">Cloud backup</a> is primary. Always.
  </p>
  <p>
    But sometimes internet is garbage. Sometimes files are huge. This holds a backup of everything important. Also great
    for sending large video files to clients without waiting six hours for uploads to finish.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ Section -->
  <h2 id="faq">Quick Answers</h2>
  <FAQSection faqs={faqs}/>

  <!-- Conclusion -->
  <h2 id="conclusion">What I've Learned</h2>
  <p>
    Go lighter than you think you should. Seriously. You can buy almost anything almost anywhere. That thing you're
    panicking about at 3am the night before your flight? Probably doesn't matter.
  </p>
  <p>
    Stuff that survives the cut is stuff you actually use every single day. The rest is just weight.
  </p>
  <p>
    And when your laptop dies somewhere without an Apple store? The spec sheet isn't going to help you then. Buy boring.
    Buy reliable.
  </p>
  <p>
    Travel light but work heavy.
  </p>
  <p>
    Planning your first trip? Check the <a href="/blog/top-destinations-digital-nomads">destination guide</a>. Get
    <a href="/blog/best-travel-insurance-remote-workers">proper travel insurance</a>. Not kidding about that last part.
  </p>

  <!-- Related Articles -->
  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
